ll  and I can only go on what you see before  you know the success of it I think it  opened over the summer and one  individual I know was up at three  o'clock in the morning with his rucksack  on waiting to come down here and since  then I think they've been here ever  since this has been a really good  community project for parents as well as  young people the whole community you've  got got behind this well I think so I  mean  I've been very impressed by the way that  seems to have happened and unsolicited  you had we had the W I decided to adopt  barroners as one of their charitably  wanted to support for a whole year so  they gave us the proceeds of their  fundraising efforts at the other end of  the scale the ashburton Family Services  who run woodlanders and the play groups  they've been really enthusiastic and  again the children were involved in  penny spirals and all sorts of  fundraising louise's and help it along  so I've been delighted and the police  and the secondary in the primary schools  everybody seems to recognize it as a  good thing I mean I won't say I'm Jamie  84 safe and everybody's always you know  one hundred percent enthusiastic but  they can see the real benefits of having  a facility like this in the outdoors for  young people you just tell me your name  and your role on the committee my name  is Francois Shelvin and I'm the vice  chair and I have been for three and a  half years since we started raising  funds for the skatepark so tell us a  little bit about what this project means  to ashburton I think it's given the  young people a place to congregate and  to do something that's healthy and  outdoors in the fresh air rather than  just sort of hanging around on street  corners being a bit of a nuisance  sometimes I'm not saying that they are a  nuisance but maybe that's what some  people's view is of young people and I  just feel like there needs really need  to be catered for and I'm just ecstatic  that they've got somewhere where the